219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Begin the session with an introduction to the concept of block programming, which you can apply to Scratch, which will be used to illustrate the process in the following sequences. This activity can be applied to all block programming software suitable for cycle 3: MakeCode, MakeCode Arcade, Scratch, Kodable, Tynker, etc. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Explain to students that programming is about giving a computer precise instructions to perform specific actions. Introduce Scratch as an educational tool that allows students to create stories, games, and animations by assembling colored blocks, like puzzle pieces.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (To further explore these concepts, play a game of Memory Scratch to help students familiarize themselves with the vocabulary and appearance of the blocks before translating them into movements. Explain that the goal is to match each Scratch block image with the word that describes it. The game consists of 32 cards, meaning 16 associations. You can vary the level of difficulty by varying the number of cards used.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Organize the class into groups and explain the rules of Memory Scratch. Students take turns turning over two cards. If they find a correct pair, they keep it and play again. The player with the most pairs at the end wins. You can also choose a collaborative game mode, the goal being to introduce them to the blocks in a fun way. )
/Pg 10 0 R
